## 1️⃣ Taming Transformers for High‑Resolution Image Synthesis  
*Paper:* `taming_transformers_for_high-resolution_image_synthesis.tex`  
*Codebase:* `CompVis/taming‑transformers` – `/workplace/taming-transformers`

### 1.1 Core Mathematical Components

| # | Symbol / Term | Formal LaTeX Definition (copy‑pasted from the paper) | Explanation |
|---|---------------|------------------------------------------------------|-------------|
| 1 | Encoder output (continuous latent) | $$\mathbf{z}_e(\mathbf{x}) = \operatorname{Encoder}(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{H\times W\times D}$$ | Convolutional‑Transformer encoder that maps an image **x** to a spatial grid of D‑dim vectors. |
| 2 | Quantization (nearest‑code‑book entry) | $$\mathbf{z}_q(\mathbf{x}) = \operatorname{Quantize}\big(\mathbf{z}_e(\mathbf{x})\big) = \mathbf{e}_{k^\*},\qquad k^\*=\arg\min_k\|\mathbf{z}_e(\mathbf{x})-\mathbf{e}_k\|_2$$ | **e_k** is a learnable embedding in the codebook **E**; the nearest one is selected. |
| 3 | Commitment loss | $$\mathcal{L}_\text{commit}= \big\|\operatorname{sg}\big[\mathbf{z}_e(\mathbf{x})\big]-\mathbf{z}_q(\mathbf{x})\big\|_2^2$$ | Forces the encoder output to stay close to the selected codebook vector. **sg** = stop‑gradient. |
| 4 | Code‑book loss | $$\mathcal{L}_\text{codebook}= \big\|\mathbf{z}_e(\mathbf{x})-\operatorname{sg}\big[\mathbf{z}_q(\mathbf{x})\big]\big\|_2^2$$ | Updates the codebook vectors toward the encoder output (via EMA in the implementation). |
| 5 | Reconstruction (pixel‑wise) loss | $$\mathcal{L}_\text{rec}= \|\mathbf{x}-\hat{\mathbf{x}}\|_2^2$$ | **\hat{x}** = Decoder( **z_q** ). |
| 6 | Perceptual loss (VGG) | $$\mathcal{L}_\text{perc}= \sum_{l}\frac{1}{N_l}\big\|\phi_l(\mathbf{x})-\phi_l(\hat{\mathbf{x}})\big\|_2^2$$ | **\phi_l** are selected VGG‑19 feature maps. |
| 7 | Adversarial GAN loss (PatchGAN) | $$\mathcal{L}_\text{GAN}= \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}}\!\big[\log D(\mathbf{x})\big] + \mathbb{E}_{\hat{\mathbf{x}}}\!\big[\log\big(1-D(\hat{\mathbf{x}})\big)\big]$$ | Standard discriminator‑generator minimax objective. |
| 8 | Full training objective | $$\mathcal{L}= \lambda_\text{rec}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{rec} + \lambda_\text{perc}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{perc} + \lambda_\text{commit}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{commit} + \lambda_\text{code}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{codebook} + \lambda_\text{GAN}\,\mathcal{L}_\text{GAN}$$ | Coefficients are set in the training script (`train.py`). |

### 1.2 Where the Equations Live in the Code

| Equation # | File (repo‑relative) | Class / Function | Key Lines |
|------------|----------------------|------------------|-----------|
| 1–2 | `modules/vqvae/quantize.py` | `VectorQuantizer` (method `forward`) | `z_q = self.embed[indices]` |
| 3 | Same file | `VectorQuantizer.loss` | `commitment_loss = ((z_e.detach() - z_q)**2).mean()` |
| 4 | Same file | `VectorQuantizer.loss` | `codebook_loss = ((z_e - z_q.detach())**2).mean()` |
| 5 | `models/autoencoder.py` | `AutoEncoder.forward` → `recon_loss = F.mse_loss(x_recon, x)` |
| 6 | `modules/perceptual.py` | `PerceptualLoss` | uses a pre‑trained VGG‑19 (`self.vgg`) and computes MSE on selected layers. |
| 7 | `models/discriminator.py` & `train.py` | `Discriminator` + `torch.nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss` | `d_loss_real = bce_logits(d_real, torch.ones_like(d_real))` |
| 8 | `train.py` | `train_one_epoch` – loss aggregation | `loss = (lambda_rec * recon) + (lambda_perc * perc) + (lambda_commit * commit) + (lambda_code * codebook) + (lambda_gan * gan)` |

